# Track 6 Item 3 Slice 3 — Typed Canary Outcome Policy — COMPLETE

Date: 2026-09-11
Branch: `build/initial-product`
Status: application GREEN; documentary checkpoint pending exact-head Windows CI at creation time.

## Purpose

Close the family-outcome-policy gap left intentionally open by Slice 2 without redesigning the reversible canary executor and without inventing unsupported causal metrics or one universal optimization score.

## Production contract

- Core: `src/FFPerformanceEngine.Core/Services/GenericGuardianTypedCanaryOutcomeEvaluator.cs`
- Interface: existing `IGenericGuardianSessionCanaryOutcomeEvaluator`
- CPU/GPU are the only generic families currently allowed to emit `Improved` or `Regressive`.
- Required before/after metrics: measured finite `TelemetryStandardMetrics.FrameFpsAverage` and `TelemetryStandardMetrics.FrameTimeAverageMs`, each with coverage >= 0.75.
- `Improved`: relative FPS gain >= 2% and average frame time does not worsen.
- `Regressive`: relative FPS loss >= 2%.
- `Inconclusive`: sub-threshold change, frame-time worsening without the defined FPS regression, missing/partial/low-coverage/invalid evidence, or any family without a dedicated typed outcome contract.
- `MemoryPressure`, `VramPressure`, `FrameTimeInstability`, `ThermalThrottling`, `NetworkInstability` intentionally remain `Inconclusive`; fallback/unavailable families also fail closed.
- The 2% boundary is reused from the existing specialized `GuardianEngine.CanaryImproved` default; 75% coverage is reused from the typed universal bottleneck analyzer causal-evidence floor.
- No universal score, new arbitrary threshold, mutation authority, persistence, learning, cooldown/Action Budget, host/startup wiring, WPF or recommendation/profile authority was added.

